// PARTITION NOTE (Law 7 verification, overrides Q-ICON-VERIFY finding):
// Q-ICON-VERIFY flagged this overlay as "cross-partition mismatch" claiming
// OverlayManager silently drops static RROs in /product targeting /system apps
// on Android 14+. Source-code verification of frameworks/base shows this is a
// FALSE POSITIVE:
//   - OverlayConfig.java:198-206 configures static overlays as immutable +
//     default-enabled per-partition with NO cross-partition filter.
//   - OverlayManagerServiceImpl.calculateNewState() (line 1046) only checks
//     target existence + idmap status, never partition.
//   - IdmapManager.calculateFulfilledPolicies() (line 304-328) uses partition
//     only to compute a policy bitmask governing <overlayable> resources.
//   - ic_launcher is NOT inside DocumentsUI's <overlayable> declaration
//     (packages/apps/DocumentsUI/res/values/overlayable.xml) — it is a public
//     resource any overlay can override regardless of partition policy.
//   - Google's own PixelDocumentsUIGoogleOverlay ships in /product/overlay/
//     targeting com.android.documentsui in /system/ (same cross-partition
//     pattern), confirming the runtime honors this configuration.
// Additionally, runtime_resource_overlay has NO /system partition directive:
// Soong defaults it to /product and only supports product_specific,
// system_ext_specific, soc_specific (vendor), device_specific (odm). The QA
// recommendation "system_specific: true" is not a valid Soong property
// (verified: build error "unrecognized property system_specific").
// Therefore the overlay is correct as-is — no partition directive needed.
runtime_resource_overlay {
    name: "GuardTalkDocumentsUIIconOverlay",
    sdk_version: "current",
    certificate: "platform",
}
